{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 10, 20]], "date-time": "2026-10-20T18:40:54Z", "timestamp": 1792521654651, "version": "4.5.0"}, "reference-count": 42, "publisher": "American Physical Society (APS)", "issue": "10", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 10, 20]], "date-time": "2026-10-20T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1792454400000}, "content-version": "vor", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}], "funder": [{"name": "European XFEL R&D program"}], "content-domain": {"domain": ["journals.aps.org"], "crossmark-restriction": true}, "short-container-title": ["Phys. Rev. Accel. Beams"], "accepted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 24]]}, "abstract": "<jats:p>We demonstrate the first realization of electron beam and x-ray pulse shaping with a corrugated wakefield structure at a pulsed-mode megahertz-repetition-rate hard x-ray free-electron laser (XFEL). This is achieved by an L-shaped corrugated structure with low-beta optics and a collimator. Variable streaking direction and signature of quadrupole wakefield compensation are observed by controlling the position of the electron beam relative to the corrugated plates. Spectral analysis at 7 keV indicates a reduction of the reconstructed x-ray pulse duration by an approximate factor of 4 compared to the standard self-amplified spontaneous emission mode of the European x-ray free-electron laser. X-ray pulses with uniform properties along a train of 100 pulses at a 1.125 MHz repetition rate are generated under 10 Hz pulse mode. These results are a step toward tailoring the temporal and spectral domain of x-ray pulses at megahertz repetition rates with high flexibility and compatibility.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1103/r4nx-blvw", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 24]], "date-time": "2026-09-24T13:25:33Z", "timestamp": 1790256333000}, "update-policy": "https://doi.org/10.1103/crossmark-policy", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Experimental d